## History of observation and exploration

The observation and exploration of Saturn can be divided into three phases: (1) pre-modern observations with the [naked eye](/wiki/Naked_eye "Naked eye"), (2) telescopic observations from Earth beginning in the 17th century, and (3) visitation by [space probes](/wiki/Space_probe "Space probe"), in orbit or on [flyby](/wiki/Planetary_flyby "Planetary flyby"). In the 21st century, telescopic observations continue from Earth (including [Earth-orbiting](/wiki/Geocentric_orbit "Geocentric orbit") [observatories](/wiki/Space_telescope "Space telescope") like the [Hubble Space Telescope](/wiki/Hubble_Space_Telescope "Hubble Space Telescope")) and, until [its 2017 retirement](/wiki/Cassini_retirement "Cassini retirement"), from the [*Cassini*](/wiki/Cassini%E2%80%93Huygens "Cassini–Huygens") orbiter around Saturn.

### Pre-telescopic observation

See also: [Saturn (mythology)](/wiki/Saturn_%28mythology%29 "Saturn (mythology)") and [Planets in astrology § Saturn](/wiki/Planets_in_astrology#Saturn "Planets in astrology")

Saturn has been known since prehistoric times,[\[138\]](#cite_note-NMM_Saturn_2-140) and in early recorded history it was a major character in various mythologies. [Babylonian astronomers](/wiki/Babylonian_astronomy "Babylonian astronomy") systematically observed and recorded the movements of Saturn.[\[139\]](#cite_note-ptrsl276_1257_43-141) In ancient Greek, the planet was known as Φαίνων *[**Phainon**](/wiki/Phaenon "Phaenon")*,[\[140\]](#cite_note-142) and in Roman times it was known as the "star of [Saturn](/wiki/Saturn_%28mythology%29 "Saturn (mythology)")" or the "star of the [Sun](/wiki/Sun "Sun") (i.e. [Helios](/wiki/Helios "Helios"))".[\[141\]](#cite_note-143)[\[142\]](#cite_note-144) In [ancient Roman mythology](/wiki/Roman_mythology "Roman mythology"), the planet Phainon was sacred to this agricultural god, from which the planet takes its modern name.[\[143\]](#cite_note-Saturn_in_ancient_mythologies-145) The Romans considered the god Saturnus the equivalent of the [Greek god](/wiki/Greek_mythology "Greek mythology") [Cronus](/wiki/Cronus "Cronus"). In modern [Greek](/wiki/Greek_language "Greek language"), the planet retains the name *Cronus*—Κρόνος: *Kronos*.[\[144\]](#cite_note-146)

The Greek scientist [Ptolemy](/wiki/Ptolemy "Ptolemy") based his calculations of Saturn's orbit on observations he made while it was in [opposition](/wiki/Opposition_%28astronomy%29 "Opposition (astronomy)").[\[145\]](#cite_note-ps04_1893_862-147) In [Hindu astrology](/wiki/Jyotisha "Jyotisha"), there are nine astrological objects, known as [Navagrahas](/wiki/Navagraha "Navagraha"). Saturn is known as "[Shani](/wiki/Shani "Shani")" and judges everyone based on the good and bad deeds performed in life.[\[143\]](#cite_note-Saturn_in_ancient_mythologies-145)[\[145\]](#cite_note-ps04_1893_862-147) Ancient [Chinese](/wiki/Chinese_astrology "Chinese astrology") and Japanese culture designated the planet Saturn as the "earth star" or "soil star" (土星). This was based on [Five Elements](/wiki/Five_elements_%28Chinese_philosophy%29 "Five elements (Chinese philosophy)") which were traditionally used to classify natural elements.[\[146\]](#cite_note-148)[\[147\]](#cite_note-149)[\[148\]](#cite_note-150)

In [Hebrew](/wiki/Hebrew_language "Hebrew language"), Saturn is called [*Shabbathai*](/wiki/Shabtai "Shabtai").[\[149\]](#cite_note-151) Its angel is [Cassiel](/wiki/Cassiel "Cassiel"). Its intelligence or beneficial spirit is ['Agȋȇl](/wiki/Agiel "Agiel") ([Hebrew](/wiki/Hebrew_language "Hebrew language"): אגיאל, [romanized](/wiki/Romanization_of_Hebrew "Romanization of Hebrew"): *ʿAgyal*),[\[150\]](#cite_note-TheMagus-152) and its darker spirit ([demon](/wiki/Demon "Demon")) is [Zȃzȇl](/wiki/Zazel_%28spirit%29 "Zazel (spirit)") ([Hebrew](/wiki/Hebrew_language "Hebrew language"): זאזל, [romanized](/wiki/Romanization_of_Hebrew "Romanization of Hebrew"): *Zazl*).[\[150\]](#cite_note-TheMagus-152)[\[151\]](#cite_note-CrystalLinks-153)[\[152\]](#cite_note-TC-20170308-154) Zazel has been described as a [great angel](/wiki/Angel "Angel"), invoked in [Solomonic magic](/wiki/Solomon#Angels_and_magic "Solomon"), who is "effective in [love conjurations](/wiki/Evocation "Evocation")".[\[153\]](#cite_note-FE-2014-155)[\[154\]](#cite_note-HP-1998-156) In [Ottoman Turkish](/wiki/Ottoman_Turkish_language "Ottoman Turkish language"), [Urdu](/wiki/Urdu "Urdu"), and [Malay](/wiki/Malay_language "Malay language"), the name of Zazel is 'Zuhal', derived from the [Arabic language](/wiki/Arabic_language "Arabic language") ([Arabic](/wiki/Arabic_language "Arabic language"): زحل, [romanized](/wiki/Romanization_of_Arabic "Romanization of Arabic"): *Zuhal*).[\[151\]](#cite_note-CrystalLinks-153)

### Telescopic pre-spaceflight observations

[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Galileo.arp.300pix.jpg/250px-Galileo.arp.300pix.jpg)](/wiki/File:Galileo.arp.300pix.jpg)

[Galileo Galilei](/wiki/Galileo_Galilei "Galileo Galilei") observed the rings of Saturn in 1610, but was unable to determine what they were.

[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Saturn_Robert_Hooke_1666.jpg/250px-Saturn_Robert_Hooke_1666.jpg)](/wiki/File:Saturn_Robert_Hooke_1666.jpg)

[Robert Hooke](/wiki/Robert_Hooke "Robert Hooke") noted the shadows (`a` and `b`) cast by both the globe and the rings on each other in this drawing of Saturn in 1666.

Saturn's rings require at least a 15-mm-diameter [telescope](/wiki/Telescope "Telescope")[\[99\]](#cite_note-binoculars-101) to resolve and were not known to exist until [Christiaan Huygens](/wiki/Christiaan_Huygens "Christiaan Huygens") saw them in 1655 and published his observations in 1659. [Galileo](/wiki/Galileo "Galileo"), with his primitive telescope in 1610,[\[155\]](#cite_note-chan2000-157)[\[156\]](#cite_note-cain2008_15390-158) incorrectly thought of Saturn's appearing not quite round as two moons on Saturn's sides.[\[157\]](#cite_note-cain2008_15418-159)[\[158\]](#cite_note-cain2008_46237-160)

When Huygens used greater telescopic magnification this notion was refuted, and the rings were truly seen for the first time. Huygens discovered Saturn's [moon Titan](/wiki/Titan_%28moon%29 "Titan (moon)"). [Giovanni Domenico Cassini](/wiki/Giovanni_Domenico_Cassini "Giovanni Domenico Cassini") later discovered four other moons: [Iapetus](/wiki/Iapetus_%28moon%29 "Iapetus (moon)"), [Rhea](/wiki/Rhea_%28moon%29 "Rhea (moon)"), [Tethys](/wiki/Tethys_%28moon%29 "Tethys (moon)"), and [Dione](/wiki/Dione_%28moon%29 "Dione (moon)"). In 1675, Cassini discovered the gap now known as the [Cassini Division](/wiki/Cassini_Division "Cassini Division").[\[159\]](#cite_note-micek2007-161)

No further discoveries of significance were made until 1789 when [William Herschel](/wiki/William_Herschel "William Herschel") discovered two further moons, [Mimas](/wiki/Mimas_%28moon%29 "Mimas (moon)") and [Enceladus](/wiki/Enceladus "Enceladus"). The irregularly shaped satellite [Hyperion](/wiki/Hyperion_%28moon%29 "Hyperion (moon)"), which has a [resonance](/wiki/Orbital_resonance "Orbital resonance") with Titan, was discovered in 1848 by a British team.[\[160\]](#cite_note-pa54_122-162)

In 1899, [William Henry Pickering](/wiki/William_Henry_Pickering "William Henry Pickering") discovered Phoebe, a highly [irregular satellite](/wiki/Irregular_satellite "Irregular satellite") that does not rotate synchronously with Saturn as the larger moons do.[\[160\]](#cite_note-pa54_122-162) Phoebe was the first such satellite found and it took more than a year to orbit Saturn in a [retrograde orbit](/wiki/Retrograde_orbit "Retrograde orbit"). During the early 20th century, research on Titan led to the confirmation in 1944 that it had a thick atmosphere—a feature unique among the Solar System's moons.[\[161\]](#cite_note-apj100_378-163)

### Spaceflight missions

Main article: [Exploration of Saturn](/wiki/Exploration_of_Saturn "Exploration of Saturn")

#### *Pioneer 11* flyby

[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/P11saturnb.jpg/250px-P11saturnb.jpg)](/wiki/File:P11saturnb.jpg)

*Pioneer 11* image of Saturn

[*Pioneer 11*](/wiki/Pioneer_11 "Pioneer 11") made the first flyby of Saturn in September 1979, when it passed within 20,000 km (12,000 mi) of the planet's cloud tops. Images were taken of the planet and a few of its moons, although their resolution was too low to discern surface detail. The spacecraft also studied Saturn's rings, revealing the thin F-ring and the fact that dark gaps in the rings are bright when viewed at a high [phase angle](/wiki/Phase_angle_%28astronomy%29 "Phase angle (astronomy)") (towards the Sun), meaning that they contain fine light-scattering material. In addition, *Pioneer 11* measured the temperature of Titan.[\[162\]](#cite_note-164)

#### *Voyager* flybys

In November 1980, the [*Voyager 1*](/wiki/Voyager_1 "Voyager 1") probe visited the Saturn system. It sent back the first high-resolution images of the planet, its rings and satellites. Surface features of various moons were seen for the first time. *Voyager 1* performed a close flyby of Titan, increasing knowledge of the atmosphere of the moon. It proved that Titan's atmosphere is impenetrable at [visible wavelengths](/wiki/Visible_wavelength "Visible wavelength"); therefore no surface details were seen. The flyby changed the spacecraft's trajectory out of the plane of the Solar System.[\[163\]](#cite_note-Voyager-165)

Almost a year later, in August 1981, [*Voyager 2*](/wiki/Voyager_2 "Voyager 2") continued the study of the Saturn system. More close-up images of Saturn's moons were acquired, as well as evidence of changes in the atmosphere and the rings. During the flyby, the probe's turnable camera platform stuck for a couple of days and some planned imaging was lost. Saturn's gravity was used to direct the spacecraft's trajectory towards Uranus.[\[163\]](#cite_note-Voyager-165)

The probes discovered and confirmed several new satellites orbiting near or within the planet's rings, as well as the small [Maxwell Gap](/wiki/Maxwell_Gap "Maxwell Gap") (a gap within the [C Ring](/wiki/Rings_of_Saturn#C_Ring "Rings of Saturn")) and [Keeler gap](/wiki/Keeler_gap "Keeler gap") (a 42 km-wide gap in the [A Ring](/wiki/A_Ring "A Ring")).[\[164\]](#cite_note-166)

#### *Cassini–Huygens* spacecraft

Main article: [*Cassini–Huygens*](/wiki/Cassini%E2%80%93Huygens "Cassini–Huygens")

[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Enceladus_geysers_June_2009.jpg/250px-Enceladus_geysers_June_2009.jpg)](/wiki/File:Enceladus_geysers_June_2009.jpg)

At Enceladus's south pole, geysers spray water from many locations along the [tiger stripes](/wiki/Tiger_stripes_%28Enceladus%29 "Tiger stripes (Enceladus)").[\[165\]](#cite_note-NASA-20140728-167)

The [*Cassini–Huygens*](/wiki/Cassini%E2%80%93Huygens "Cassini–Huygens") [space probe](/wiki/Space_probe "Space probe") entered orbit around Saturn on 1 July 2004. In June 2004, it conducted a close flyby of [Phoebe](/wiki/Phoebe_%28moon%29 "Phoebe (moon)"), sending back high-resolution images and data. *Cassini*'s flyby of Saturn's largest moon, Titan, captured radar images of large lakes and their coastlines with numerous islands and mountains. The orbiter completed two Titan flybys before releasing the [*Huygens* probe](/wiki/Huygens_%28spacecraft%29 "Huygens (spacecraft)") on 25 December 2004. *Huygens* descended onto the surface of Titan on 14 January 2005.[\[166\]](#cite_note-nature438_7069_758-168)

Starting in early 2005, scientists used *Cassini* to track lightning on Saturn. The power of the lightning is approximately 1,000 times that of lightning on Earth.[\[167\]](#cite_note-169)

In 2006, NASA reported that *Cassini* had found evidence of liquid [water reservoirs](/wiki/Reservoir "Reservoir") no more than tens of meters below the surface that erupt in [geysers](/wiki/Geyser "Geyser") on Saturn's moon [Enceladus](/wiki/Enceladus_%28moon%29 "Enceladus (moon)"). These jets of icy particles are emitted into orbit around Saturn from vents in the moon's south polar region.[\[168\]](#cite_note-170) Over 100 geysers have been identified on Enceladus.[\[165\]](#cite_note-NASA-20140728-167) In May 2011, NASA scientists reported that Enceladus "is emerging as the most habitable spot beyond Earth in the Solar System for life as we know it".[\[169\]](#cite_note-171)[\[170\]](#cite_note-172)

[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Saturn_eclipse.jpg/250px-Saturn_eclipse.jpg)](/wiki/File:Saturn_eclipse.jpg)

Saturn eclipses the Sun, as seen from [*Cassini*](/wiki/Cassini%E2%80%93Huygens "Cassini–Huygens"). The rings are visible, including the [F Ring](/wiki/F_Ring "F Ring").

*Cassini* photographs have revealed a previously undiscovered planetary ring, outside the brighter main rings of Saturn and inside the G and E rings. The source of this ring is hypothesized to be the crashing of a meteoroid off [Janus](/wiki/Janus_%28moon%29 "Janus (moon)") and [Epimetheus](/wiki/Epimetheus_%28moon%29 "Epimetheus (moon)").[\[171\]](#cite_note-173) In July 2006, images were returned of hydrocarbon lakes near Titan's north pole, the presence of which were confirmed in January 2007. In March 2007, hydrocarbon seas were found near the North pole, the largest of which is almost the size of the [Caspian Sea](/wiki/Caspian_Sea "Caspian Sea").[\[172\]](#cite_note-174) In October 2006, the probe detected an 8,000 km (5,000 mi) diameter cyclone-like storm with an eyewall at Saturn's south pole.[\[173\]](#cite_note-175)

From 2004 to 2 November 2009, the probe discovered and confirmed eight new satellites.[\[174\]](#cite_note-176) In April 2013, *Cassini* sent back images of a hurricane at the planet's north pole 20 times larger than those found on Earth, with winds faster than 530 km/h (330 mph).[\[175\]](#cite_note-177) On 15 September 2017, the *Cassini–Huygens* spacecraft performed the "Grand Finale" of its mission: a number of passes through gaps between Saturn and Saturn's inner rings.[\[176\]](#cite_note-NASA-20170915-178)[\[177\]](#cite_note-NYT-20170914-179) The [atmospheric entry](/wiki/Atmospheric_entry "Atmospheric entry") of *Cassini* ended the mission.

#### Possible future missions

The continued exploration of Saturn is still considered to be a viable option for NASA as part of their ongoing [New Frontiers program](/wiki/New_Frontiers_program "New Frontiers program") of missions. NASA previously requested for plans to be put forward for a mission to Saturn that included the [Saturn Atmospheric Entry Probe](/wiki/Saturn_Atmospheric_Entry_Probe "Saturn Atmospheric Entry Probe"), and possible investigations into the habitability and possible discovery of life on Saturn's moons Titan and Enceladus by [*Dragonfly*](/wiki/Dragonfly_%28Titan_space_probe%29 "Dragonfly (Titan space probe)").[\[178\]](#cite_note-180)[\[179\]](#cite_note-181)

## In fiction

Main article: [Saturn in fiction](/wiki/Saturn_in_fiction "Saturn in fiction")

Saturn has frequently appeared in fiction since at least 1752, when [Voltaire](/wiki/Voltaire "Voltaire") published his novel [*Micromégas*](/wiki/Microm%C3%A9gas "Micromégas").[\[180\]](#cite_note-GreenwoodOuterPlanets-182) Early works generally depicted it as solid,[\[181\]](#cite_note-WestfahlSaturn-183) whereas Saturn is later correctly described as a gaseous planet. Saturn's moons are also featured in fiction, especially [Titan](/wiki/Titan_%28moon%29 "Titan (moon)").[\[182\]](#cite_note-StablefordSaturn-184)

